\chapter{Shared Test Fixtures}%
\label{conftest}

Several CLI modules integrate with each other at runtime.
For example, [[todo start]] delegates to the [[track]]
module to begin time tracking (\cref{cli.todo,cli.track}).
This means that tests for one module can inadvertently
write to another module's real storage if only its own
paths are patched.

Rather than duplicating the patching logic in every test
file, we collect the shared isolation in a single
[[conftest.py]] that pytest discovers automatically.
It has to guard two rather different kinds of leakage.

\begin{description}
\item[Per-test storage] Tracking data lives in files whose
  paths a fixture can patch for the duration of one test.
  [[temp_tracking_dir]] does that; any test module whose
  commands touch tracking data can request it---either
  directly or via fixture composition (as
  [[temp_todo_dir]] does).
\item[The persistent configuration] Course registers,
  tracking preferences and form definitions live in one
  [[typerconf]] configuration file shared by the whole
  process.  No fixture can protect it, because test
  modules write to it while they are being \emph{imported}
  (\cref{sec:isolating-the-config}).  Once the file has
  been moved out of harm's way, fixtures take over again:
  one hands a test back the configuration it started with,
  the other lets a test check that the move still holds
  (\cref{sec:config-fixtures}).
\end{description}

The order of the module body follows that split: the
configuration must be redirected before the first
[[nytid]] import, whereas the fixtures only have to exist
before the first test runs.

<<[[conftest.py]]>>=
"""Shared pytest isolation for CLI tests.

Redirects the persistent configuration to a throw-away
directory and provides temporary-directory fixtures that
patch storage functions, so tests never touch real user
data.

Manual debugging outside pytest must apply the same
patching or point `track.data_dir` at a temporary
location first; otherwise ad-hoc commands will use
`~/.nytid/tracking`.
"""
import pathlib
import tempfile
import pytest
from unittest.mock import patch

import sys
sys.path.insert(0, "../src")

<<redirect the persistent config into a sandbox>>

from nytid.cli.track import (
    get_tracking_dir,
    get_tracking_data_file,
    get_current_session_file,
    get_current_session_lock_file,
    get_tracking_lock_file,
    sanitize_who,
    default_username,
)


<<tracking fixture>>

<<configuration fixtures>>
@

\section{Isolating the persistent configuration}%
\label{sec:isolating-the-config}

[[typerconf]] derives the location of its configuration
file from [[sys.argv[0]]]: under pytest that is the
[[pytest]] executable, so the whole test suite reads and
writes [[~/.config/pytest/config.json]].  That file is
real, it is the user's, and it survives the session.

The consequences are not hypothetical.  A test that adds
a course register and forgets to remove it leaves a name
in that file pointing at a [[mkdtemp]] directory; once
the system reclaims the directory, \emph{every later
session} starts with a register whose path does not
exist, and the failure shows up in a test that never
touched the registry---long after the run that caused it,
which is what makes such a failure look intermittent.
The [[try]]--[[finally]] blocks in the test modules are
only half a defence: they rely on every author
remembering.

A pytest fixture cannot close the hole, not even a
session-scoped autouse one.  Fixtures run when the first
test runs, and by then the damage is done: several test
modules invoke [[registry add]] at \emph{module scope}
to build their harness (\cref{sec:courses-test-harness}),
which pytest executes during collection.  The redirection
therefore has to happen while [[conftest.py]] itself is
being imported---pytest imports it before it imports any
test module---and before this file imports anything from
[[nytid]].

\subsection{The sandbox}

Each session gets its own empty directory, so nothing
carries over from the previous run and a leaked register
dies with the session that created it.

<<redirect the persistent config into a sandbox>>=
import atexit
import shutil
import typerconf

CONFIG_SANDBOX = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="nytid-tests-")
atexit.register(shutil.rmtree, CONFIG_SANDBOX,
                ignore_errors=True)
@

Before we take the real configuration path away from
[[typerconf]], we write it down.  The redirection below is
a promise about a file that nothing in the suite otherwise
names, and an unnamed file is one no test can check: the
suite would go on passing whether the promise held or not.
Recording the path turns it into something a test can
assert about (\cref{sec:config-fixtures}).

The reading has to happen \emph{here}, above the
redirection---noweb concatenates the definitions of this
chunk in source order, so a block written further down the
file would run after [[typerconf.dirs]] had been replaced
and would faithfully record the sandbox as the real
configuration.

<<redirect the persistent config into a sandbox>>=
REAL_CONFIG_FILE = (
    pathlib.Path(typerconf.dirs.user_config_dir)
    / "config.json"
)
@

\subsection{Redirecting \texttt{typerconf}}

[[typerconf]] computes [[dirs]] once at import time and
then bakes the resulting path into the \emph{default
arguments} of [[Config.read_config]] and
[[Config.write_config]].  A default argument is evaluated
when the [[def]] executes, so by the time we get control
the path is already captured in each function's
[[__defaults__]] tuple.  Reassigning [[typerconf.dirs]]
alone changes nothing, and neither does patching [[HOME]]
or [[XDG_CONFIG_HOME]]---those are read before we are.
We have to overwrite the captured value itself.

Doing that blindly would be a poor trade: if a future
[[typerconf]] moves the path out of the first default
slot, the assignment would quietly succeed and the suite
would go back to writing the user's real config.  So we
check what we are about to replace and refuse to guess.

<<redirect the persistent config into a sandbox>>=
def redirect_config_default(method, conf_file):
    """Point `method`'s captured default config path at
    `conf_file`."""
    defaults = method.__defaults__
    if not defaults or not str(defaults[0]).endswith(
        "config.json"
    ):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"typerconf.{method.__qualname__} no longer "
            f"captures its config path as its first "
            f"default argument; the test suite's "
            f"isolation needs updating"
        )
    method.__defaults__ = (conf_file,) + defaults[1:]
@

We still replace [[typerconf.dirs]], for the code that
has not been written yet.  Any attribute of a
[[platformdirs.AppDirs]] names a directory, so a
stand-in that answers every [[*_dir]] query with the
sandbox keeps future lookups inside it rather than
letting one escape unnoticed.

<<redirect the persistent config into a sandbox>>=
class SandboxDirs:
    """Stand-in for `platformdirs.AppDirs` that resolves
    every directory to one sandbox."""

    def __init__(self, root):
        self.root = str(root)

    def __getattr__(self, name):
        if name.endswith("_dir"):
            return self.root
        raise AttributeError(name)


typerconf.dirs = SandboxDirs(CONFIG_SANDBOX)
redirect_config_default(
    typerconf.Config.read_config,
    str(pathlib.Path(CONFIG_SANDBOX) / "config.json"),
)
redirect_config_default(
    typerconf.Config.write_config,
    str(pathlib.Path(CONFIG_SANDBOX) / "config.json"),
)
@

\section{Fixtures for the sandboxed configuration}%
\label{sec:config-fixtures}

The redirection settles \emph{where} configuration writes
land.  It says nothing about what one test leaves in that
file for the next one, nor about how a future refactoring
of this module would be caught if it aimed the writes back
at the user.  A fixture answers each question.

\subsection{Undoing a test's configuration writes}

[[typerconf.set]] is not a surgical edit.  It reads the
whole configuration, changes one value in the parsed
structure and writes the whole file back, so the file is
rewritten even when the change is a no-op.  Setting a value
to [[None]] deletes the key---but only the key: the
dictionaries created along the way to it stay.  A test that
sets [[track.debug]] and dutifully sets it back to [[None]]
therefore hands the next test a configuration containing
[[{"track": {}}]].

That leftover is harmless in itself.  What is not harmless
is the pattern that produced it: undoing a write key by
key, correctly, in every test that makes one.  It is the
same weakness as the registry's [[try]]--[[finally]] blocks
(\cref{sec:isolating-the-config})---a defence that depends
on each author remembering, and that a [[set]] with a path
one level deeper defeats again.

Putting the file's bytes back undoes the write whatever
shape it took, and a fixture's teardown runs even when the
test body fails, which a [[finally]] in the test only does
if the author wrote one.  We yield the path as well, so a
test that wants to look at what it wrote does not have to
reconstruct it.

<<configuration fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture
def preserve_config():
    """Yield the sandboxed config file, restoring its
    contents afterwards."""
    conf_file = (
        pathlib.Path(CONFIG_SANDBOX) / "config.json"
    )
    if conf_file.exists():
        before = conf_file.read_bytes()
    else:
        before = None

    yield conf_file

    if before is None:
        conf_file.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    else:
        conf_file.write_bytes(before)
@

\subsection{Checking that the redirection holds}

The one thing this module must never do is write the
user's own configuration, and that is precisely the
outcome no ordinary test notices: the suite passes exactly
as well when the writes go to the real file.  A test can
only tell the two apart by looking at the real file
itself---which is why we recorded its path before
redirecting.

We compare a digest and a modification time rather than the
file's contents.  Between them they also catch the write
that happens to reproduce the file byte for byte---the
timestamp moves where the digest does not---and neither
puts the user's real configuration into the output of a
failing assertion.

<<configuration fixtures>>=
import hashlib


@pytest.fixture
def real_config_fingerprint():
    """Return a callable that fingerprints the user's real
    config file, or returns None if it doesn't exist."""

    def fingerprint():
        try:
            content = REAL_CONFIG_FILE.read_bytes()
        except (FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):
            return None
        return (
            REAL_CONFIG_FILE.stat().st_mtime_ns,
            hashlib.sha256(content).hexdigest(),
        )

    return fingerprint
@

\section{Tracking Storage Fixture}

Per-worker session files (see \cref{cli.track}) live
under a single directory, with filenames derived from the
worker name via [[sanitize_who]].  The fixture must patch
all path-resolution functions so that \emph{all}
tracking I/O---session files, lock files, and the
cumulative data file---lands inside a temporary
directory.

We use [[side_effect]] for
[[get_current_session_file]] and
[[get_current_session_lock_file]] because they are called
with a [[who]] argument that varies per invocation,
whereas the history paths are nullary and can use a fixed
[[return_value]].

We point [[get_tmux_state_dir]] at the \emph{same}
temporary directory as [[get_tracking_dir]].  Production
separates the two (persistent tracking data versus
tmux-scoped runtime state), but most tests exercise one
side only and need not care where the boundary runs.
Tests of the migration between the two locations patch
[[get_tracking_dir]] again with a second directory.  The
real [[get_tmux_state_dir]] guarantees its directory
exists, so the fixture creates the temporary directory up
front to honor the same contract.

We also patch [[get_tmux_server_generation]], for a
different kind of isolation: the tmux server key embeds
the server's PID and start time, which the real helper
obtains by querying the running tmux server.  Tests must
be hermetic and deterministic, so the fixture pins the
generation to the value baked into the test scaffold's
[[TEST_SERVER_KEY]] ([[123-1700000000]]).  Since the
generation comes from the server, not from [[$TMUX]], the
PID fields in tests' fake [[$TMUX]] values need not match
it.  Tests that need a different answer---or that assert
the helper is \emph{not} called---simply patch it again;
the innermost patch wins.

<<tracking fixture>>=
@pytest.fixture
def temp_tracking_dir():
    """Create a temporary directory for tracking data
    during tests.

    Patches storage functions so that per-worker session
    files resolve inside the temporary directory.
    """
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmpdir:
        temp_dir = (
            pathlib.Path(tmpdir) / "tracking"
        )
        temp_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        temp_data_file = (
            temp_dir / "tracking_data.json"
        )
        temp_history_lock = (
            temp_dir / "tracking_data.lock"
        )

        def _session_file(who=None):
            if who is None:
                who = default_username
            suffix = sanitize_who(who)
            return temp_dir / (
                f"current_session_{suffix}.json"
            )

        def _session_lock_file(who=None):
            return _session_file(who).with_suffix(
                ".lock"
            )

        with patch(
            "nytid.cli.track.get_tracking_dir",
            return_value=temp_dir,
        ), patch(
            "nytid.cli.track.get_tmux_state_dir",
            return_value=temp_dir,
        ), patch(
            "nytid.cli.track"
            ".get_tmux_server_generation",
            return_value="123-1700000000",
        ), patch(
            "nytid.cli.track"
            ".get_tracking_data_file",
            return_value=temp_data_file,
        ), patch(
            "nytid.cli.track"
            ".get_current_session_file",
            side_effect=_session_file,
        ), patch(
            "nytid.cli.track"
            ".get_current_session_lock_file",
            side_effect=_session_lock_file,
        ), patch(
            "nytid.cli.track"
            ".get_tracking_lock_file",
            return_value=temp_history_lock,
        ):
            yield temp_dir
@
